                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_imbalance:     `<strong>That's the diagnosis Seijts would recognise.</strong> Vos isn't low on character everywhere — his Drive and Courage are real. The problem is <em>imbalance</em>: unchecked by Temperance, Humility, Justice and Collaboration, those strengths tip into recklessness and harm. Now you can develop the brake, not rebuild the man.`,
                    coach_r1_deficit:       `<strong>“Low on character across the board” misses it.</strong> A flat-deficit read ignores the eleven-quarter streak and the real Drive and Courage behind it. The insight is imbalance — strength becoming vice — not absence. Name which dimensions are over-played and which are deficient.`,
                    coach_r1_competence:    `<strong>This isn't a competence gap.</strong> He has the skills — he's beaten quota eleven straight quarters. Reframing conduct as “he lacks skills” lets everyone avoid the character data that actually predicts the next failure at a bigger scale.`,
                    coach_r1_motivation:    `<strong>Incentives won't fix this.</strong> He is maximally motivated — that's part of the problem. A motivation framing dodges the behavioural evidence on Temperance, Humility and Justice. Assess the character, don't reach for a bonus lever.`,
                    coach_r1_wrongoverplay: `<strong>Humility and Justice aren't his over-plays.</strong> The evidence — bulldozed peers, the off-policy deal, the engagement gap — points the other way. His over-played strengths are Drive and Courage running without a brake.`,
                    coach_r1_nooverplay:    `<strong>“Just a strong all-round leader” is the board's blind spot.</strong> If nothing is over-played, why the regretted attrition, the skip-level complaint and the off-policy deal? The whole point is that a strength unbalanced becomes a liability. Name it.`,
                    coach_r1_integrity:     `<strong>Careful labelling the off-policy deal as pure dishonesty.</strong> Integrity may be involved, but the dominant pattern is Temperance, Humility, Collaboration and Justice — the brake, the self-awareness and the regard for peers. Over-narrowing to Integrity mis-targets the intervention.`,
                    coach_r1_wrongdef:      `<strong>Transcendence and Humanity aren't the evidenced deficits.</strong> Re-read the verbatims: recklessness, arrogance and harm to peers point at Temperance, Humility, Collaboration and Justice. Cite the behaviour, name the dimension.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp, defensible audit.</strong> Imbalance named, the over-played Drive and Courage identified, and the deficient Temperance, Humility, Collaboration and Justice cited from behaviour. That is character assessed like a competency — actionable and defensible to a compensation committee.`,
                    coach_r1_vague:         `<strong>“He has a character problem” is not an assessment — it's a shrug.</strong> Which dimensions? Show the evidence that Temperance is deficient and Drive is over-played. Without the dimension and the behaviour you can't design an intervention, measure change, or defend any of this to a committee — or, later, a lawyer.`,
                    coach_r2_temphum:       `<strong>Good prioritisation.</strong> Temperance is the brake and Humility the self-awareness — develop those two and the recklessness and arrogance ease. You can't develop ten dimensions at once; the discipline is the few with the highest leverage.`,
                    coach_r2_temphumcol:    `<strong>Strong, targeted set.</strong> Temperance, Humility and Collaboration go straight at the recklessness, the arrogance and the harm to peers — the three highest-leverage dimensions for this case. That's targeted character development, not a personality rebuild.`,
                    coach_r2_allten:        `<strong>Ten priorities is no priority.</strong> You can't develop the whole character constellation at once on this budget and timeline. Pick the two or three dimensions that most reduce risk and raise effectiveness, and aim the intervention there.`,
                    coach_r2_drive:         `<strong>Doubling down on Drive and Courage is exactly the failure mode.</strong> Those are the over-played strengths already tipping into recklessness. Develop the brake — Temperance, Humility — not the accelerator that's already harming the org.`,
                    coach_r2_hold:          `<strong>Develop-but-hold is the defensible call.</strong> You don't promote a reckless leader to all-revenue scale on faith, and you don't exit your top producer untested. Hold the CRO decision pending measured evidence of change — and define what change would justify it.`,
                    coach_r2_manageout:     `<strong>You've decided to exit your top producer without testing developability.</strong> 38% of new ARR walks with him. Seijts's whole point is that character can be developed and measured. Did you run the audit to diagnose, or to justify a verdict you'd already reached?`,
                    coach_r2_promote:       `<strong>You've put all of Meridian's revenue and 600 people under him because he beats quota.</strong> His org runs 22 engagement points below average and loses reps at double the rate — and you're about to multiply his span of control. You rewarded the number and overruled the character data that predicts the next failure at a bigger scale.`,
                    coach_r2_noarr:         `<strong>You left the 38% ARR concentration unaddressed.</strong> Whatever you decide about Vos, if he leaves — pushed or pulled by that competitor — you have a revenue cliff regardless of whether the character call was right. The talent decision and the business-risk decision are inseparable.`,
                    coach_r3_coaching:      `<strong>Coaching can target Temperance and Humility directly.</strong> A focused executive-coaching engagement is a credible, well-instrumented lever for the brake and the self-awareness — provided you wrap it in measurement and a consequence, and stay inside the budget.`,
                    coach_r3_cycle360:      `<strong>A structured 360-and-accountability cycle is high-leverage and measurable.</strong> It re-assesses the character indicators against a baseline and builds accountability in — exactly what treating character like a competency requires. Keep it in budget and define the consequence.`,
                    coach_r3_stretch:       `<strong>A stretch with conduct guardrails develops in the real role.</strong> It can lift effectiveness, but make sure the guardrails and the measurement actually bite on Temperance and Collaboration — a stretch alone can just reward the behaviour you're trying to curb.`,
                    coach_r3_peershadow:    `<strong>Peer-shadowing is cheap and humane but light on its own.</strong> It nudges Humility and Collaboration, but for an SVP heading toward CRO it may be under-powered. Pair it with measurement and a consequence or it won't move the gap enough.`,
                    coach_r3_sprawl:        `<strong>Coaching, a full 360, an offsite, a mentor AND a leadership program — for one executive.</strong> That's not a plan, it's a wish-list, and you've tripled a CA$120,000 budget. Development isn't volume; it's leverage. Which single intervention, aimed at which two dimensions, gives the most gap-closure per dollar?`,
                    coach_r3_overbudget:    `<strong>You've blown the CA$120,000 cap.</strong> The committee will not approve an over-budget development plan for one leader pre-IPO — and an unfunded plan is no plan. Re-scope to one high-leverage intervention that fits the cap.`,
                    coach_r3_fullmeasure:   `<strong>That's development built like a competency.</strong> One well-scoped intervention in budget, a baseline, a 6-month re-assessment and a real consequence if nothing changes. Now you can tell the board whether it worked — and act on the answer.`,
                    coach_r3_nomeasure:     `<strong>“Coach him on collaboration” — over what horizon, measured how?</strong> Without a baseline, a review date and a consequence, you haven't built a development plan, you've bought him time. What gets measured here, and what happens in six months if nothing's changed?`,
                    coach_r3_capitulate:    `<strong>Softening the plan and fast-tracking the offer is capture, not retention.</strong> The moment the standard bends to the threat, the audit was theatre. You de-risk the ARR by spreading relationships, not by surrendering the conduct standard to keep him.`,
                    coach_r3_ultimatum:     `<strong>An ultimatum makes the revenue cliff real.</strong> Picking a fight with a defensive star who's being courted by a competitor is how 38% of new ARR walks out the door. Hold the standard, but name the support — confrontation without development just loses you the producer and the lesson.`,
                    coach_r4_perfdata:      `<strong>That's the business case.</strong> Character framed as leading-indicator performance and governance data — the attrition, the engagement gap, the compliance exposure, the IPO due-diligence stakes — turns “HR opinion” into board-grade evidence. That's how you win a results-obsessed chair.`,
                    coach_r4_softskill:     `<strong>“The right thing to do” won't survive this chair.</strong> A values-only frame cedes the argument to “but he hits his number.” Make character measurable performance and governance data — the attrition, engagement and compliance costs — or the board discounts it.`,
                    coach_r4_numbers:       `<strong>Leading with the quota record concedes the whole case.</strong> If the character read is a footnote to the number, you've reproduced the very blind spot that created Daniel Vos. The number is exactly why the character data matters most right now.`,
                    coach_r4_watchlist:     `<strong>An informal watch-list catches no one in time.</strong> Without character built into the promotion gates, the next high-performing, reckless leader sails through on results — just like Vos did. Institutionalise the audit or you'll run this fire drill again.`,
                    coach_r4_oneoff:        `<strong>Treating this as a one-off guarantees a repeat.</strong> The system rewarded results and ignored conduct for years — that's what produced Vos. If nothing changes in the talent process, the next “Daniel Vos” is already being promoted.`,
                    coach_r4_measurable:    `<strong>Board-ready and defensible.</strong> You made character measurable performance and governance data, built it into the promotion gates, and defended it with baseline, behaviours, horizon and consequence. That's character treated as a performance-and-governance investment — exactly the argument that lands pre-IPO.`,
                    coach_r4_hedge:         `<strong>Hedging hands the decision to the chair.</strong> “It's a judgment call” is what every results-first board wants to hear — it lets them promote the number. If your audit is rigorous, defend it as measurable and developable, don't concede it's a matter of taste.`,
                    coach_r4_retreat:       `<strong>Retreating undoes the entire audit.</strong> Conceding that results outweigh the character concerns is how the org got here. You ran a rigorous, defensible assessment — abandoning it at the committee tells every future leader the number is all that's measured.`,

