                    insight_measured:       `Character measured and developed: you converted anecdote into a dimensional profile, prioritised the highest-leverage dimensions, funded one instrumented intervention within the CA$120k cap, and defended it as performance and governance data. The character gap is closing, the intervention fits, and the retention risk is under control.`,
                    insight_cliff:          `You made a defensible character call but left a revenue cliff: retention risk is still high because the 38% ARR concentration wasn't de-risked. Even a correct people decision is dangerous if the institution remains hostage to one person's relationships.`,
                    insight_partial:        `A partial diagnosis: you got some of the audit right but didn't fully close the gap. Re-examine whether you named the imbalance at the dimension level, prioritised the right two or three, and instrumented the intervention with a real consequence.`,
                    insight_verdict:        `This reads as a verdict, not an assessment. The character gap barely closed — most likely you mis-diagnosed the imbalance, rewarded the number, or built development with no measurement. Character treated like a competency would have moved these KPIs.`,
                    insight_vague:          `Your audit leaned on a vague verdict rather than dimension-by-dimension evidence. “He has a character problem” cannot be developed, measured or defended — name the dimension and cite the behaviour.`,
                    insight_blown:          `You blew the CA$120,000 budget with intervention sprawl. Development is leverage, not volume — one well-targeted, well-instrumented intervention beats a wish-list the board won't fund.`,
                    insight_lowfit:         `Intervention fit is low: the lever, the dimensions and the budget didn't line up, or the measurement was missing. A development plan needs a baseline, a horizon and a consequence to be more than buying time.`,
                    insight_highrisk:       `Retention risk stayed high. Whether through an ultimatum, a promote-or-exit overreach, or leaving the 38% ARR concentration unaddressed, the institution is still exposed to losing Vos and his accounts.`,
                    insight_governance:     `You made the governance case: character as leading-indicator performance data, embedded in the promotion gates so the next reckless star is caught earlier. Pre-IPO, that is exactly the maturity bankers and public investors look for.`,

                    hint_blown:             `You've overshot the CA$120,000 cap. Re-scope to one high-leverage intervention — coaching or a structured 360 cycle — that fits the budget; the board won't fund a wish-list.`,
                    hint_vague:             `Your diagnosis is too vague to act on. Name the specific dimensions — over-played Drive and Courage, deficient Temperance, Humility, Collaboration, Justice — and cite the behaviours.`,
                    hint_risk:              `Retention risk is high. De-risk the 38% ARR concentration and hold the conduct standard with support rather than ultimatums, so losing Vos doesn't become a revenue cliff.`,
                    hint_fit:               `Intervention fit is weak. Pick one lever aimed at the priority dimensions, keep it in budget, and instrument it with a baseline, a 6-month horizon and a consequence.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — the gap is closing, the intervention fits and retention risk is controlled. Now defend it to the board as performance and governance data and embed it in the promotion process.`,

